      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a memo for a client and I need to clarify the main differences between ETFs and mutual funds. Can you give me a technical breakdown of how they differ in terms of liquidity and management style? A table would be helpful.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "| Feature | Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs) | Mutual Funds |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Liquidity | Highly liquid | Less liquid, as investors buy and sell shares through the mutual fund company |\n| Management Style | Typically passive, designed to track an index | Can be either passive or actively managed |\n\nETFs offer higher liquidity than mutual funds since they can be bought and sold throughout the trading day at market prices. This trading capability is facilitated by their standard listing on stock exchanges. On the other hand, mutual funds are less liquid; investors can only buy or sell their shares at the end of the trading day, or during the next scheduled sale, at the net asset value (NAV) determined by the fund's portfolio. As for management style, ETFs tend to have a passive management approach, usually designed to closely replicate the performance of a particular index. In contrast, mutual funds can vary, with some aiming to passively mirror index performance and others using an active management strategy to try to outperform the market.",
